Title:
Windows Telemetry (Process-Related ETW Providers and Events)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post catalogs Windows telemetry generated about process activity, focusing on ETW providers and the specific event IDs/fields that reveal what a process is doing. It highlights “relevant” events across key providers such as Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Process, Kernel-Audit-API-Calls, Kernel-File, and Kernel-Network. The Threat-Intelligence provider section is especially geared toward detecting common injection and tampering primitives (VirtualAlloc/Protect/MapView, Read/WriteProcessMemory, QueueUserAPC, SetThreadContext, suspend/resume, token impersonation, syscall usage) and includes rich context like VAD metadata and signature/protection levels. Kernel-Process events cover process/thread lifecycle and module load/unload, while Kernel-File and Kernel-Network provide file creation/name events and TCP/UDP connection/traffic telemetry. This is useful for both Red Teamers (to understand what actions are observable) and Blue Team/DFIR engineers (to build detections and investigations around high-signal kernel/ETW events). It’s interesting because it maps low-level behaviors to concrete ETW event schemas and arguments, enabling more precise hunting and correlation.

Technical Focus:
- ETW providers and event schemas (GUIDs, event symbol names, arguments)
- Process injection telemetry (AllocVM/ProtectVM/MapView, APC, SetThreadContext)
- Cross-process memory operations (ReadVM/WriteVM) with VAD enrichment
- Process/thread lifecycle and image load telemetry (Kernel-Process)
- Kernel audit of sensitive API calls (OpenProcess/OpenThread, symbolic links)
- Kernel file and network telemetry (file name/create, TCP/UDP connection events)

Use Cases:
- Build detections for process injection and remote thread/APC techniques using Threat-Intelligence ETW
- Correlate process start/stop, thread start/stop, and ImageLoad to reconstruct execution chains
- Hunt for suspicious cross-process memory reads/writes and abnormal memory protection changes (RWX, etc.)
- Monitor file creation/name events to identify dropped payloads or staging artifacts
- Attribute outbound C2 attempts via kernel TCP/UDP connection telemetry tied to PID
- Validate red team tradecraft against what Windows can natively log at kernel/ETW level

Keywords:
Windows telemetry, ETW, Event Tracing for Windows, Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Process, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Audit-API-Calls, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-File, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Network, process injection, VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect, MapViewOfSection, QueueUserAPC, SetThreadContext, ReadProcessMemory, WriteProcessMemory, VAD, image load, thread start, token impersonation, syscall usage, TCP connection attempted, UDP send/receive